00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:49.989
So quick introductions.

00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:51.030
My name's Jeremy Johnson.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.030
I'm a product manager on
the cloud networking team.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:01:02.850
And with me today I've got
Patrick Ancillotti from Vimeo.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:07.180
And Patrick runs both
security and systems at Viemo.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.380
He's a VP of Systems
and Security.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.160
And to his left we've
got Kyle Moschetto.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.300
He's the Director of Dev
Ops and Security for Bitly.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.990
And very similar role.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:27.510
Runs all of systems
and security at Bitly.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:31.650
And to his left one of my
colleagues, Olivier Vautrin.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:36.550
He is a product manager in
cloud networking team as well.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:37.050
And

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.780
So with not much further ado,
I'm going to get started.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:44.130
So when we think about IP
addressing in the cloud,

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.320
one of the things
that strikes me often,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.290
because this is
my area of focus,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:54.060
is that the last noN-elastic
resource in cloud

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.620
is the IP address.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.450
Usually when you're
out of IP addresses,

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.850
you're out of IP addresses, and
it can cause a real problem.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.704
And so this is why
we thought this

00:02:02.704 --> 00:02:04.620
would be a useful
conversation today because I

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:09.090
think we all struggle with
some issues with IP addresses.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.150
We kind of break this
down into two categories.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.895
So public IP addresses,
so IP addresses

00:02:13.895 --> 00:02:15.270
that have access
to the internet,

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:19.170
and private IP addresses,
generally our RFC 1918 space.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.840
So we're going to start by
talking about how we think

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.270
about private address space.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.480
And I talk to Patrick
quite a bit about this,

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.440
and Patrick's got a
pretty unique viewpoint

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:34.380
on the differences between
private and public IP

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.100
addresses or the lack thereof.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.285
So I'll just hand
it over to Patrick.

00:02:38.285 --> 00:02:40.410
PATRICK ANCILLOTTI: Can
everyone hear me all right?

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:43.315
I can hear me, so I'm
assuming you can hear me.

00:02:43.315 --> 00:02:45.690
One of the things that's a
little different about the way

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.560
we treat IP addressing is
to us, functionally, there's

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.462
no difference between a
private and a public address.

00:02:53.462 --> 00:02:54.920
Public addresses
face the internet,

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.810
but there is a
technological difference.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.630
But we don't inherently
trust any network.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:05.380
So for us, it's about explicit
instead of implicit security.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:07.530
We go out of our
way to make sure

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.750
that even if something is
talking over the internet,

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:13.290
there's encryption, there
is explicit firewall rules

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.570
that allow this thing
to talk to this thing

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:19.860
instead of saying this network
that we trust is secure

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:21.250
is able to talk to us.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.170
And so we spend a lot
of time thinking about--

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:29.620
if any of you've watched any
of the Beyond Corp. talks,

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.840
think about it like
a Beyond Corp. style

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.960
behavior for network
security in the cloud.

00:03:37.904 --> 00:03:39.570
Google behaves this
way internally, too.

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:41.910
There's no Google service
that talks to another Google

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:44.040
service, even over a
Google network, that

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.790
is unencrypted and isn't
explicitly allowed to do so.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:51.490
Did I get that right?

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:52.365
JEREMY JOHNSON: Yeah.

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:56.400
And I think Kyle has a slightly
different viewpoint on this.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:57.900
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Yeah.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.010
Our environment's a
little less complicated

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:00.551
than the Vimeo
environment, and so we

00:04:00.551 --> 00:04:04.170
take a much more simple approach
to running a single VPC.

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:08.074
We use our IP address range in
the private space in full RFC.

00:04:08.074 --> 00:04:09.990
And we have to map and
balance that across our

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.520
on prem solutions across
direct interconnect.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:15.600
And our public IP address
concerns only revolve around

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.954
a single class C. That is the
root of all of the problems

00:04:18.954 --> 00:04:20.954
that we face at Bitly
from a public perspective,

00:04:20.954 --> 00:04:22.600
and we'll get into
more of that later.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:26.660
But we keep an implicit
trust within our VPC.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.920
So if you're in our
VPC, you have trust

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.367
with some obvious
basic firewall rules

00:04:31.367 --> 00:04:32.950
to talk to just about
everything else.

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:37.410
So it simplifies our CIC
pipeline and other things.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:38.285
OLIVIER VAUTRIN: Yes.

00:04:38.285 --> 00:04:40.618
So we see that we have really
two different models here.

00:04:40.618 --> 00:04:43.300
So on Vimeo side,
there's no trust

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:47.270
inside VPC, explicit
security for VPC

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:49.370
between each VM inside a policy.

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:53.540
And in Bitly side, you have the
limit of trust, which is VPC.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:57.530
And inside this limit
of trust, everything

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:00.017
can connect to everything.

00:05:00.017 --> 00:05:00.850
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Yes.

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:02.360
For some limited
aspects, but yeah,

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:03.230
everything can
connect to everything.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:05.720
We put a lot of our security
focus at the border,

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.880
and then inside we realize
that if you've already

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:10.670
broken the door there, you're
going to get around for now.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:11.586
OLIVIER VAUTRIN: Yeah.

00:05:11.586 --> 00:05:18.020
So when we designed the solution
for Google cloud, in our mind

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:20.450
the VPC is limit of trust.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:25.010
And we designed two
models for team security.

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:28.700
So there's a shared VPC that
we introduced last year.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:32.550
In shared VPC, if you
have multiple teams,

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:36.050
all those teams are
going to be in one VPC.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:37.750
You have a
centralized team doing

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:41.240
the management, the security
management for all those teams.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.610
So a centralized
team of network admin

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:46.070
doing the management
for all those teams.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:48.040
But each team can be
in different project

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:50.770
inside this VPC, and
so have different IM

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:54.590
rules or different billing.

00:05:54.590 --> 00:06:00.530
The other model is VPC per team.

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:03.080
When you have one VPC
per team, then each team

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.810
will have different network
admin and different IM,

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:08.960
different billing, but
different security rules.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:12.500
And to connect them, you
can use VPC peering that we

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:15.290
introduced last year as well.

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:20.730
You can use VPN, and you
can use VM multi NIC.

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:22.516
You can also have
multi NIC and NVM.

00:06:22.516 --> 00:06:25.957
I think you are using
VPC peering, right?

00:06:25.957 --> 00:06:28.040
PATRICK ANCILLOTTI: Yeah,
we're using VPC peering.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:30.700
We enabled it pretty early on.

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:31.920
We're doing one of--

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.970
I'm on my third cloud migration
of the last 18 months.

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:36.860
So I've got a lot
of gray hair now.

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:38.896
I'm a lot sadder.

00:06:38.896 --> 00:06:40.430
But during one of
the migrations,

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:43.940
we take the stance that
each functional unit

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:47.221
should have both a development
project and a production

00:06:47.221 --> 00:06:47.720
project.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:49.136
And one of the
production projects

00:06:49.136 --> 00:06:51.470
needed to speak private--

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:54.680
we weren't able to add
encryption to the protocol.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.830
So we opted to use VPC peering
because it at least gave us

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:00.120
some--

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.370
it moved us towards the
aspiration of everything

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:04.250
needing to be encrypted.

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:06.750
So with VPC peering, there
were a lot of advantages,

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:09.480
but there were some hiccups
that we ended up bumping into,

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:11.540
which is--

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:14.970
we built a tool around solving
some of these problems.

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:16.470
JEREMY JOHNSON: So
this is where I'd

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:19.140
like to ask the
audience some questions.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:21.060
So how many folks
in the audience--

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:23.910
we've talked about the
idea of implicit trust

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:25.280
versus explicit trust.

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:27.270
The implicit trust
model would really

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:30.540
leverage that shared
VPC and host project,

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:33.660
whereas the explicit
trust model might

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:37.860
prefer to use separate VPCs and
VPC peering or the multi NIC

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:38.770
approach.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:42.340
So how many folks in here use
more of the shared VPC model?

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:45.900
Awesome.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:49.110
It looks like maybe about
slightly less than half

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:51.180
of the room.

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:55.500
And how many folks
use a model where

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.090
you're using explicit trust?

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:03.020
It's kind of not quite the rest.

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:05.085
PATRICK ANCILLOTTI:
You guys are better.

00:08:05.085 --> 00:08:05.960
JEREMY JOHNSON: Yeah.

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:07.260
There are a couple of both.

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:09.915
There are some that
didn't raise your hands.

00:08:09.915 --> 00:08:12.290
So I suspect those that didn't
raise their hands probably

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:14.690
are using maybe
smaller deployments

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:17.400
with a single project.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:17.900
All right.

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:19.520
Let's move on.

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:21.590
So one of the questions
we also ask ourselves

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:25.220
while we're designing these
systems is kind of why bother

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:27.980
with IP addresses at all?

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:30.260
Obviously we need IP
addresses on the host.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:33.740
But to solve some of
that elasticity problem

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:36.720
and some of the programmability
problems that we have,

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:41.120
especially when you have
explicit rule sets to manage,

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:45.080
it might make sense to provide
an abstraction, a label, a tag,

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:48.380
something like that, as
opposed to just an IP address.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:52.580
So we do support some of
these concepts in our cloud.

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:54.570
And so I'm going to hand
it over to the panel,

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:57.279
and we're going to talk
about abstractions now.

00:08:57.279 --> 00:08:58.570
PATRICK ANCILLOTTI: I'll start.

00:08:58.570 --> 00:09:02.740
So one of the hardest documents
that we've ever tried to write

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.940
were early in our
first cloud migration.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:08.390
We have a data center,
and the data center

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:09.800
has static IP addressing.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:14.600
And we've got, as Jeremy
calls it, the number one IP

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.840
management tool in the
world, Google Sheets.

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:21.260
And we're updating the
sheet, and we start

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:23.160
doing the same in GKE because--

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:28.124
not GKE, in GCP because machines
are going to be stateful.

00:09:28.124 --> 00:09:29.540
We're just doing
a lift and shift,

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:31.730
and then we're
going to reorganize.

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:33.800
And after a couple
of weeks, we start

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:36.500
to notice that no one is
updating the IP address

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:38.720
management tool.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:41.570
And things are starting to
appear in the environment,

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:44.270
but nothing's overlapping
because the IP addresses

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:46.760
are all enforced.

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:48.860
And then we start to
deploy GKE, and everything

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:50.420
became completely trashed.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:54.140
So GKE is wonderful, but if any
of you have ever deployed it,

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:56.180
every container gets an IP.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:59.000
There is no way
you're going to build

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:04.090
any sort of human-involved
IP address management

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:05.520
with that in mind.

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:10.910
And a lot of the time we
spent doing that was more--

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:14.840
we kept trying to lift concepts
from our on prem data centers

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:18.560
and from our offices and on
prem locations into the cloud,

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:21.620
and being like, this
shit just sort of work.

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:22.920
And we all know better.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:24.250
We should have known better.

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:29.050
But slowly but surely we
realized IP addressing is not

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:31.000
actually that important to us.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:32.620
It seems to only
come up for us when

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:34.000
we want to overlap
with something

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:35.980
that is in a physical space.

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:39.190
And we've worked around
it in other ways.

00:10:41.165 --> 00:10:42.790
KYLE MOSCHETTO: My
end goal is to never

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:44.510
care about IP addresses again.

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:47.230
We're actively involved
in a lift and shift

00:10:47.230 --> 00:10:49.880
of a lot of our infrastructure
into GCP right now.

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:51.286
And so we have to use them.

00:10:51.286 --> 00:10:53.410
We have to use IP addresses
for our firewall rules,

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:55.180
for our [INAUDIBLE],, for the
direct interconnects that we

00:10:55.180 --> 00:10:56.957
have between data
centers and GCP.

00:10:56.957 --> 00:10:58.540
That's a requirement
because we're not

00:10:58.540 --> 00:10:59.750
going to get to
go anywhere else,

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:02.166
and we're not going to get any
security unless we do that.

00:11:02.166 --> 00:11:04.360
But as we're putting
products inside of GCP,

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:06.760
tags are pretty much
becoming the best thing

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:08.300
under the sun for us.

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:11.440
It's logical, it's easy,
it scales really well.

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:13.210
You want to touch
it after a time.

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:16.030
And I'm going to throw
a softball question over

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:17.697
to you, which is we
had a lot of trouble

00:11:17.697 --> 00:11:19.155
figuring this out
at the beginning.

00:11:19.155 --> 00:11:20.890
Can you explain the
functional difference

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:23.225
between tags, labels, and
maybe service accounts?

00:11:23.225 --> 00:11:24.100
JEREMY JOHNSON: Yeah.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:25.090
I think--

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:25.790
KYLE MOSCHETTO:
This is important.

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:26.890
This is really important.

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:26.950
JEREMY JOHNSON:
This is something

00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:28.570
that Olivier really focuses on.

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:30.186
Let's have Olivier--

00:11:30.186 --> 00:11:32.170
OLIVIER VAUTRIN: So
it has a label, tags,

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:37.300
and service accounts
that are used to group IP

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:39.370
address [INAUDIBLE] together.

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:42.400
Labels are only
used for billing,

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:45.970
so they are not used at all
for security or networking.

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:47.830
So labels, only billing.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:51.550
So for security and grouping
mechanism for networking.

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:54.577
We have tags and
service accounts.

00:11:54.577 --> 00:11:56.160
So the big difference
between the two,

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:59.040
so service accounts is a
bit more recent than tags.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:01.022
I think tags was really
two, three years ago,

00:12:01.022 --> 00:12:02.230
I guess, something like this.

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:05.980
And service accounts last year.

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:09.070
The big difference
is for tags, you

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:13.375
can have multiple tags per VM.

00:12:13.375 --> 00:12:14.750
For service accounts
you can only

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:17.457
have one service account per VM.

00:12:17.457 --> 00:12:18.290
So that's one limit.

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:21.400
The advantage of
service accounts,

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:25.280
though, is seen as a bit
more secure because service

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:28.050
accounts, you can design
IM rules to decide

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:32.315
who can add this service
account to this VM.

00:12:32.315 --> 00:12:33.690
So you can be a
bit more granular

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:37.780
into who can change your
service accounts all for VM.

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:39.390
So that's a big difference.

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:42.320
But they are used the same way.

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:45.210
They are used to group
VM together for firewalls

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:46.920
and stuff like this.

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:53.890
There's just one point on
getting rid of IP addresses.

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:57.850
So I have a bit of
an issue with that.

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:00.450
So I'm hearing a lot
from customers that oh,

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:02.970
let's get rid of IP
address and use label,

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:06.090
and I want to connect
red with blue,

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:08.310
but blue cannot be
connected with green.

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:12.150
Let's get rid of IP addresses
and use color or label

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:13.540
for all of this.

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:15.520
There's two issues
I see with that.

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:18.750
And I would love to get
your point of view on that.

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:25.590
The first one is there's always
a limit of trust somewhere,

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:27.420
so if you manage
everything, then

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:30.870
it's very easy to color your
source with those labels

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:33.010
or with those tags and so on.

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:36.630
But if you receive routes
from somebody else,

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:38.430
you cannot trust those labels.

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:41.370
If you receive PCP routes,
or if you're doing peering

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:44.700
and you're receiving some
traffic from this other

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:47.520
network, you cannot trust it.

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:49.620
So there need to
be a way, a policy

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:53.130
to map those labels
to those IP address.

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:56.490
So I don't see those IP
address disappearing,

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:59.940
even if it would be a nice goal.

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:03.520
And then that could be, perhaps,
an implementation issue.

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:06.240
So that's something we could
first find a work around

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:06.780
for that.

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:09.210
But as a scaling
issue, in a sense

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:13.810
that if you have
one label per VM

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:15.870
and you have a firewall
for them, basically

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:19.830
you have one firewall00
if you have 10 VM,

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:23.380
you have 10 firewall
in the implementation.

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:24.820
[INAUDIBLE] slash 32.

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:28.260
While IP addresses,
you can aggregate them.

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:30.450
You can do slash 24, slash 20.

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:34.900
And so with one rule, like
200 VM or something like this.

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:37.879
So there's scaling advantage
also in IP addresses.

00:14:42.824 --> 00:14:44.865
KYLE MOSCHETTO: So we need
subnet masks for tags.

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:50.120
Coming soon from Google.

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:52.380
OLIVIER VAUTRIN: Or perhaps
for smaller management,

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:55.025
for smaller customer, it
might work better than

00:14:55.025 --> 00:14:56.660
for larger deployment,
for example.

00:14:56.660 --> 00:15:02.690
So we need to find a way to
use the flexibility of tags

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:04.060
and still have the scaling.

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:04.935
JEREMY JOHNSON: Yeah.

00:15:04.935 --> 00:15:09.100
Something we use internally
at Google that is also useful

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:11.366
is sometimes we identify things.

00:15:11.366 --> 00:15:13.865
You think about what you use
IP addresses for-- you use them

00:15:13.865 --> 00:15:19.100
to apply policy, you use
them to secure communications

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:21.330
between two entities.

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:24.170
But you also use them
for identification.

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:27.320
That's primarily why they're
used in GKE, for example.

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:29.330
And one of the things
we do inside of Google

00:15:29.330 --> 00:15:31.610
is we actually use a
tuple that's the IP

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:34.130
address and a process ID.

00:15:34.130 --> 00:15:35.660
And something that
we would probably

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:38.120
extend to this kind
of pattern also

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:41.540
works probably well with
the lower order bits on V6.

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:44.330
We'll talk about
that in a minute.

00:15:44.330 --> 00:15:47.210
Before we get into
this, really quick,

00:15:47.210 --> 00:15:51.320
just a poll of the crowd
again, so loosen up.

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:55.985
How many of you folks are using
tags in your deployments today?

00:15:55.985 --> 00:15:56.610
Of, it's great.

00:15:56.610 --> 00:15:58.900
That's almost everybody.

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:03.010
How many folks have used service
accounts in your deployments?

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:03.910
Also a good number.

00:16:03.910 --> 00:16:06.020
That's almost all the room.

00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:08.630
That's great.

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:10.600
So by the way, I'd
encourage you--

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:12.600
if we're talking about
something that you really

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:17.800
want to talk about, walk up
to the mic and ask a question.

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:21.929
I think we're going
to move on into--

00:16:21.929 --> 00:16:23.470
so Patrick mentioned
something that's

00:16:23.470 --> 00:16:27.980
near and dear to my heart, which
is the IP address management.

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:29.590
This is a challenge
that we all have.

00:16:29.590 --> 00:16:32.770
And I think that IP
address managers were not

00:16:32.770 --> 00:16:34.930
built for clouds.

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:37.977
Almost saying IP,
anything about IP

00:16:37.977 --> 00:16:39.560
wasn't really built
for clouds, right?

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:41.770
We have overlapping name spaces.

00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:44.560
We have poorly
coordinated deployments

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:47.620
where developer A isn't
talking to developer B

00:16:47.620 --> 00:16:52.040
and they both end up on subnet
A. This happens all the time.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:53.990
So how do we avoid
these kinds of things?

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:58.390
We really actually need a
good IP address management

00:16:58.390 --> 00:17:03.220
system that's really built
for cloud deployments.

00:17:03.220 --> 00:17:08.800
And so we have to move
beyond spreadsheets.

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:11.214
So before we talk to
the panel about this,

00:17:11.214 --> 00:17:12.630
how many of the
folks in the crowd

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:16.231
are using spreadsheets
to manage IP addresses?

00:17:16.231 --> 00:17:16.730
OK.

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:18.960
The honest ones that raised
their hands is about half

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:20.355
of the room.

00:17:20.355 --> 00:17:21.980
And how many folks
in the room actually

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:27.859
have a commercial off-the-shelf
or home-built IPAM solution?

00:17:27.859 --> 00:17:28.359
OK.

00:17:28.359 --> 00:17:30.050
A little less than half.

00:17:30.050 --> 00:17:32.312
So again, there are
some people who just

00:17:32.312 --> 00:17:33.520
aren't managing IP addresses.

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:37.330
OLIVIER VAUTRIN: And
Jeremy, also let's

00:17:37.330 --> 00:17:38.520
say that they're using IPAM.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:43.379
Who is using those IPAM
to manage cloud resources?

00:17:43.379 --> 00:17:44.670
JEREMY JOHNSON: Only a handful.

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:49.445
AUDIENCE: Our
subnets are in IPAM.

00:17:49.445 --> 00:17:52.735
But [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:52.735 --> 00:17:53.610
JEREMY JOHNSON: Yeah.

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:58.130
So subnets are in IPAM, but
not managing individual IP

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:00.450
addresses.

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:03.240
AUDIENCE: I mean, it
depends on the environment.

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:05.851
[INAUDIBLE]

00:18:05.851 --> 00:18:07.600
JEREMY JOHNSON: This
is exactly the point.

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:12.300
So we're talking about this
idea that IP addresses in cloud

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:14.580
are oftentimes ephemeral,
especially as we

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:16.560
move towards micro services.

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:22.380
So what kind of human-driven
IPAM helps with that?

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:23.880
So I'm going to
kick it to the panel

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:26.375
and get some thoughts on this.

00:18:26.375 --> 00:18:27.750
PATRICK ANCILLOTTI:
So I probably

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:29.624
have to start with some
organizationnal stuff

00:18:29.624 --> 00:18:31.950
to explain how I
got to where I am.

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:35.070
So Vimeo practices
engineering autonomy,

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:38.137
so if a team would
like to progress faster

00:18:38.137 --> 00:18:39.720
than the rest of the
organization can,

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:41.730
they have the freedom to do so.

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:44.460
Sometimes that results in some
stuff that wasn't planned for,

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:48.010
but isn't the worst of, but
also isn't the greatest of.

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:50.430
So we, in our biggest
production project,

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:52.260
we use auto IP addressing.

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:54.297
Is everyone familiar
with when you

00:18:54.297 --> 00:18:56.130
create a network it
gives you a bunch of IPs

00:18:56.130 --> 00:18:59.940
and they overlap
everywhere all the time?

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:03.480
And once production services
are living on a network,

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:05.940
that's where they will
stay until you die.

00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:09.822
So in a lot of cases, we've
ended up using auto subnetting,

00:19:09.822 --> 00:19:11.280
not because we
planned it that way,

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:15.990
because truthfully, we didn't,
but because the progress

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:20.910
to the cloud happened
before we had infrastructure

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:23.890
folk involved
earlier on, which is

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:25.140
a great thing for the company.

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:27.720
And we still
wholeheartedly encourage

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:30.720
engineers to move quickly.

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:33.660
But it's something that that's
really hard for us to undo.

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:35.400
And at least I,
for one, would love

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:38.250
to have something where I could,
organizationally or somewhere

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:40.770
within GCP, define
available ranges

00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:43.500
and allow networks to
be created out of those,

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:45.630
and reserve them,
sort of like in IPAM.

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:47.850
But I don't have any physical
infrastructure anymore.

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:52.000
So it's easy for me to say that
without having to worry about

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:53.084
the on prem stuff anymore.

00:19:53.084 --> 00:19:55.249
JEREMY JOHNSON: Let me ask
one question of the crowd

00:19:55.249 --> 00:19:55.990
really quick.

00:19:55.990 --> 00:20:00.910
Is the distinction between auto
mode and custom mode subnects

00:20:00.910 --> 00:20:03.850
clear to everyone?

00:20:03.850 --> 00:20:07.120
OK, I see a few
shaking yes, a few no.

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:13.240
So I'll just explain at a
high level really quickly.

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:14.980
When you create a
subnet in a VPC,

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:18.430
you can have a VPC in
auto mode, in which case

00:20:18.430 --> 00:20:23.080
every region will have its
own subnets just automatically

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:25.870
allocated for you.

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:29.620
The custom mode allows you
to choose your own range

00:20:29.620 --> 00:20:33.020
and set your own subnet mask.

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:36.160
So the thing that's
interesting about this

00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:39.970
is if you've got developer
autonomy and the ability

00:20:39.970 --> 00:20:44.050
to build a VPC on your
own, you might really

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:48.130
like that auto mode
because you don't really

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:50.890
want to think about IP
addresses when you build out

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:53.240
your project.

00:20:53.240 --> 00:20:57.190
On the other hand, if you do
have an IP address policy,

00:20:57.190 --> 00:21:00.490
and you do have concerns about
talking to other organizations

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:04.830
with your project, et cetera,
then you need that custom mode.

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:06.520
The distinction is
even more important

00:21:06.520 --> 00:21:09.280
when you realize that in order
to convert from auto mode

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:12.340
to custom mode, you have
to drain the auto mode

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:15.340
VPC, which means you kind
of got to start over.

00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:18.220
So it's one of those
choices that you make

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:21.060
that has lasting implications.

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:22.810
I think that's what
Patrick is getting at.

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:24.520
They did a lot with
auto mode, and they

00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:26.444
don't want to go backwards.

00:21:26.444 --> 00:21:27.610
We're always moving forward.

00:21:32.449 --> 00:21:34.740
KYLE MOSCHETTO: So naturally
we started with auto mode,

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:37.260
and then realized really quickly
that it, of course, overlapped

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:38.400
with our private
IP address scheme

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:39.670
that we had in our data center.

00:21:39.670 --> 00:21:40.800
So we had to start back over.

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:42.210
And we also heard some
great horror stories

00:21:42.210 --> 00:21:44.126
from our product team
and our [? TAMs ?] about

00:21:44.126 --> 00:21:47.240
why we shouldn't use auto mode.

00:21:47.240 --> 00:21:50.030
So that was always fun to
hear other people's pain.

00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:52.659
But as it comes to our
address management,

00:21:52.659 --> 00:21:53.700
we're using spreadsheets.

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:55.908
We're using Google Sheets
to track our IPs right now.

00:21:55.908 --> 00:21:58.046
And our hope is that,
as we move forward,

00:21:58.046 --> 00:21:59.670
we're going to need
to do that anymore.

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:02.819
And Jeremy said IP addresses
weren't really designed

00:22:02.819 --> 00:22:04.360
for the cloud, and
I agree with that.

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:06.109
And I that if you're
spinning up thousands

00:22:06.109 --> 00:22:08.010
of containers every
day, every hour,

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:10.200
there's no human
readable format that's

00:22:10.200 --> 00:22:11.400
going to be useful to you.

00:22:11.400 --> 00:22:13.150
The only thing you can
trust at that point

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:15.510
is what your API tells you
is correct this microsecond

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:16.650
or millisecond.

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:19.470
And because of that,
organizations and process

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:21.330
and policy has to
change about how you're

00:22:21.330 --> 00:22:22.740
choosing to manage those.

00:22:22.740 --> 00:22:24.690
And maybe ephemeral
is not the right word

00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:26.661
for how I think IP
address management has

00:22:26.661 --> 00:22:27.660
to happen in the future.

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:29.850
But it's something near there.

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:31.740
The IP address becomes
this arbitrary thing

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:33.390
that just exists
in the code for you

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:35.265
and controls certain
access to certain thing.

00:22:35.265 --> 00:22:37.082
And ideally in a
cloud environment,

00:22:37.082 --> 00:22:38.790
that's programmatically
done through tags

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:40.620
or some other
thing where you can

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:44.320
come in and set that defaultly.

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:49.590
OLIVIER VAUTRIN: So one
thing that we think is--

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:53.470
a typical IPAM
don't work in cloud.

00:22:53.470 --> 00:22:56.940
So one reason for that is--

00:22:56.940 --> 00:23:01.550
one feature of an IPAM is the
mapping of IP address to DNS,

00:23:01.550 --> 00:23:02.690
to the DNS address.

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:05.610
And that is the [INAUDIBLE].

00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:10.320
So in that sense, we are
doing already big feature

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:11.940
from [INAUDIBLE] IPAM.

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:14.500
So an IPAM for cloud need to
be very different from and IPAM

00:23:14.500 --> 00:23:18.870
that could be used on prem.

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:20.700
One quick question for Patrick.

00:23:20.700 --> 00:23:26.820
So I know that you have a
tool to manage your security.

00:23:26.820 --> 00:23:28.730
Do you mind discussing
a bit about that,

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:31.329
and why had to build the
tool in the first place?

00:23:31.329 --> 00:23:32.370
PATRICK ANCILLOTTI: Sure.

00:23:32.370 --> 00:23:37.310
I think-- [INTPERPOSING VOICES]

00:23:37.310 --> 00:23:39.210
AUDIENCE: So you're
talking a lot about IPAM

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:40.994
in terms of managing
individual addresses.

00:23:40.994 --> 00:23:42.660
And this gentleman
was talking about how

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:44.770
he's actually managing
it at the subnet level.

00:23:44.770 --> 00:23:46.978
So one of the things we're
getting really heavy in on

00:23:46.978 --> 00:23:49.980
is using Kubernetes
on shared VPC, which

00:23:49.980 --> 00:23:51.720
means it has IP
aliasing, which means

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:54.690
I've got three subnets
per kubernetes cluster,

00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:55.462
plus Google.

00:23:55.462 --> 00:23:57.420
And we're going private
cluster, so then Google

00:23:57.420 --> 00:23:59.969
picks a fourth range that
they're going to peer in.

00:23:59.969 --> 00:24:01.510
And then there's
your own internal IP

00:24:01.510 --> 00:24:03.010
space, things like
Cloud SQL, seems

00:24:03.010 --> 00:24:04.259
like there's a lot of peering.

00:24:04.259 --> 00:24:06.900
So it's almost more of a
comment than a question.

00:24:06.900 --> 00:24:08.790
And it feels like there's
still an IP space,

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:11.081
because my spreadsheet is
pretty big, but it's subnets.

00:24:11.081 --> 00:24:13.257
And it's is really trying
to manage assignment,

00:24:13.257 --> 00:24:15.840
because I'll have four to five
subnets just for the Kubernetes

00:24:15.840 --> 00:24:16.860
cluster.

00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:17.740
And keeping track.

00:24:17.740 --> 00:24:20.340
And some of these are like
peered spaced, can't overlap.

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:22.372
And Google reserves
some of the one sub

00:24:22.372 --> 00:24:25.500
and two space for its
peering, its magic.

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:27.390
A tool, I think,
would still help

00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:29.940
to manage that level of
stuff, because that itself

00:24:29.940 --> 00:24:32.275
is starting to feel like its
going to get overwhelming.

00:24:32.275 --> 00:24:33.720
JEREMY JOHNSON: Right.

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:36.390
This is something that
I was going to bring up.

00:24:36.390 --> 00:24:40.080
The use of IP
addresses in Kubernetes

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:46.260
is an area that we get
a lot of concern about.

00:24:46.260 --> 00:24:50.730
And I would recommend that
anyone who is concerned with

00:24:50.730 --> 00:24:53.730
that topic should go--there's
a talk over at the Palace Hotel

00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:57.930
today at 11:40, I believe,
that's enterprise Kubernetes.

00:24:57.930 --> 00:24:59.840
I'd highly encourage
you to go watch that.

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:01.298
Or if you can't
make it over there,

00:25:01.298 --> 00:25:03.510
just find the YouTube
video after the conference

00:25:03.510 --> 00:25:06.270
because they're going to be
talking about some things.

00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:10.140
We're introducing some concepts
like custom mode Kubernetes

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:11.190
IP addressing.

00:25:11.190 --> 00:25:14.010
And so to a certain
extent, when you

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:17.490
build when you build
your GKE cluster,

00:25:17.490 --> 00:25:20.760
you're getting the
auto mode treatment.

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:25.080
And we recognize that
that's not exactly--

00:25:25.080 --> 00:25:27.750
we did custom mode
in VPC for a reason.

00:25:27.750 --> 00:25:30.240
We felt like, OK, well,
it's probably about time

00:25:30.240 --> 00:25:32.860
we did custom mode
for GKE as well.

00:25:32.860 --> 00:25:35.710
And so there's going to be some
more information about that.

00:25:35.710 --> 00:25:41.160
But at a higher level, some
of the things that happens--

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:44.430
every pod gets its own slash 24.

00:25:44.430 --> 00:25:51.200
We found that there that nominal
number of pods per machine

00:25:51.200 --> 00:25:54.720
or per node is
usually around eight.

00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:57.810
And so a slash 24 is
a little excessive.

00:25:57.810 --> 00:26:01.792
So we want to be able to
more or less right size that.

00:26:01.792 --> 00:26:03.750
So we're adding some
feature features for that,

00:26:03.750 --> 00:26:06.610
and there's a lot more
information about that today

00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:08.465
at that enterprise Kubernetes.

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:14.340
AUDIENCE: So I guess that's been
one of our biggest challenges.

00:26:14.340 --> 00:26:18.150
We have a development team
that went in with GKE,

00:26:18.150 --> 00:26:20.830
and we kind of told them, well,
OK, we'll let you do this.

00:26:20.830 --> 00:26:25.710
And then all of a sudden they
decide to do 10.0.0.0/14,

00:26:25.710 --> 00:26:28.980
which is pretty much the
rest of our enterprise,

00:26:28.980 --> 00:26:33.010
and kind of had us do some
trickery with VPN and not

00:26:33.010 --> 00:26:37.460
propagate that stuff down to
kind of give some segmentation.

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:41.544
But I know like, for example,
there's IP masquerading.

00:26:41.544 --> 00:26:43.960
But that kind of goes almost
completely against everything

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:46.601
that Kubernetes does because
I remember talking to them.

00:26:46.601 --> 00:26:48.100
I was like, yeah,
why don't you NAT?

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:49.930
And they're like, we
don't want to NAT.

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:54.830
So I started kicking
around the ideas--

00:26:54.830 --> 00:26:57.820
I know this is the big no-no for
all the people in here who are

00:26:57.820 --> 00:27:00.610
doing IP, but IPv6 because--

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:03.330
JEREMY JOHNSON: We're going
to hold that topic to the end.

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:07.150
So I have a lot to say
about that, hopefully

00:27:07.150 --> 00:27:09.140
a lot to say about that.

00:27:09.140 --> 00:27:12.310
But yeah, I do think
IPv6 sexism is important.

00:27:12.310 --> 00:27:15.940
Let's hear about
Patrick's tool real quick.

00:27:15.940 --> 00:27:19.030
PATRICK ANCILLOTTI: So when
we were converting from Kyle

00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:20.710
to where we are now--

00:27:20.710 --> 00:27:22.870
I'm a little shorter.

00:27:22.870 --> 00:27:24.970
So one of the things
that we found,

00:27:24.970 --> 00:27:26.530
there were a lot
of times we wanted

00:27:26.530 --> 00:27:28.750
to talk to things outside.

00:27:28.750 --> 00:27:32.581
And there wasn't a great way to
control the firewall run rule

00:27:32.581 --> 00:27:33.080
management.

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:35.230
So we're big Fastly customers.

00:27:35.230 --> 00:27:36.670
Being able to take
Fastly's ranges

00:27:36.670 --> 00:27:41.890
and say, extending our explicit
security behaviors to the edge,

00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:46.210
we want only these and ranges
to talk to these five origin

00:27:46.210 --> 00:27:50.440
servers, or this group of origin
servers, or this GKE cluster.

00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:52.240
And if we extended
it further, we

00:27:52.240 --> 00:27:54.610
wanted to be able to say
when a GKE cluster comes up,

00:27:54.610 --> 00:27:56.740
it can speak to this
database server.

00:27:56.740 --> 00:27:59.960
And so we built, for all
intents and purposes,

00:27:59.960 --> 00:28:01.480
a firewall rule
convergence tool.

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:05.170
So it watches all of the
assorted infrastructures,

00:28:05.170 --> 00:28:08.920
our AWS stuff, our GCP
stuff, our Fastly stuff.

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:11.050
And it has a conflict
file that converges

00:28:11.050 --> 00:28:14.340
into API-driven firewall rules.

00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:18.190
So it'll go and say, oh,
I see a new instance.

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:21.610
Within a minute it
catches the event,

00:28:21.610 --> 00:28:23.920
it creates the firewall
rule, updates all of the ones

00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:25.141
it needs to.

00:28:25.141 --> 00:28:27.640
And so we're able to dynamically
generate firewall rule map.

00:28:27.640 --> 00:28:29.860
So when we talk about
explicit security,

00:28:29.860 --> 00:28:32.680
if I was doing it all by
hand, it just wouldn't happen.

00:28:32.680 --> 00:28:34.300
Engineers are lazy.

00:28:34.300 --> 00:28:35.840
That's why we're engineers.

00:28:35.840 --> 00:28:38.330
And so we call it
a border guard.

00:28:38.330 --> 00:28:41.120
We're going to be open
sourcing it at some point.

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:47.320
But it allows us to care even
less about IP addressing,

00:28:47.320 --> 00:28:50.010
because we really care
about entity security.

00:28:50.010 --> 00:28:52.129
And so some of the
things we could easily

00:28:52.129 --> 00:28:53.170
do with service accounts.

00:28:53.170 --> 00:28:59.260
We could inter application
or inter virtual machine

00:28:59.260 --> 00:29:00.430
communication.

00:29:00.430 --> 00:29:04.930
But as soon as we extend past
what is a VPC boundary or past

00:29:04.930 --> 00:29:08.050
what is a project
boundary, those

00:29:08.050 --> 00:29:12.620
tend to stop being
viable for us.

00:29:12.620 --> 00:29:13.740
JEREMY JOHNSON: Yeah.

00:29:13.740 --> 00:29:19.530
So just to kind of summarize
this topic, I think with cloud,

00:29:19.530 --> 00:29:23.400
and when we start talking
about infrastructure as code,

00:29:23.400 --> 00:29:29.580
we have to be able to manage IP
addresses more based on intent

00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:35.550
than on strict policy that
is enforced by humans.

00:29:35.550 --> 00:29:37.530
And so what Patrick's
team has done

00:29:37.530 --> 00:29:40.680
is they've been building
this tool for a while

00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:41.720
to automate all that.

00:29:41.720 --> 00:29:43.440
And as he said,
you're not going to be

00:29:43.440 --> 00:29:46.950
able to get to this
level of explicit trust

00:29:46.950 --> 00:29:49.640
without an infrastructure
as code model.

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:52.260
There's just no way that
you're going to get there.

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:56.670
So things might look
bad to a certain extent.

00:29:56.670 --> 00:29:58.590
But also, given
the fact that we're

00:29:58.590 --> 00:30:02.007
opening APIs to be able to
automate our infrastructure

00:30:02.007 --> 00:30:03.840
nowadays, we are actually
getting to a place

00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:08.640
where we can do more
better faster and stay more

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:10.480
on top of what's going on.

00:30:10.480 --> 00:30:16.170
I think my answer to Olivier's
objection about the cloud IPAM

00:30:16.170 --> 00:30:19.980
not dealing with, maybe,
DNS or the GCP stuff

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:22.860
that you may not have as
much control over in cloud

00:30:22.860 --> 00:30:27.930
is that actually, I look
at IP address management

00:30:27.930 --> 00:30:30.450
less as a product and
more as a set of features

00:30:30.450 --> 00:30:31.410
or a set of functions.

00:30:31.410 --> 00:30:34.690
And those are things that
roll out as APIs over time.

00:30:34.690 --> 00:30:39.600
And those APIs will
facilitate the kind of tools

00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:41.490
that Patrick is
putting together.

00:30:41.490 --> 00:30:45.600
If you are on the explicit
security model with IP address

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:48.960
management I would encourage
you to look at their tool,

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:49.770
watch this space.

00:30:49.770 --> 00:30:52.320
I think it's called
Border Guard is

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:54.180
the tool that they're building.

00:30:54.180 --> 00:30:58.230
And it will be open sourced
at some point, correct?

00:30:58.230 --> 00:31:00.480
PATRICK ANCILLOTTI: It also
has a really neat feature.

00:31:03.030 --> 00:31:06.030
Again, engineering autonomy.

00:31:06.030 --> 00:31:07.770
One of my engineers
get a little annoyed

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:10.320
with finding random
firewall rules popping up

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:12.450
because explicit
security makes people's

00:31:12.450 --> 00:31:15.775
lives a little more difficult.
And so now it has nuke mode.

00:31:15.775 --> 00:31:17.650
It will go through all
the rules and be like,

00:31:17.650 --> 00:31:21.629
I don't about these,
delete it, which helps.

00:31:21.629 --> 00:31:22.420
JEREMY JOHNSON: OK.

00:31:22.420 --> 00:31:24.720
We're going to shift gears
one more time and talk

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:26.830
about public IP addresses.

00:31:26.830 --> 00:31:30.870
It's not as controversial
a topic in a lot of cases

00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:36.150
because for enterprises, the
public IP address is primarily

00:31:36.150 --> 00:31:42.090
used to go fetch updates to
your operating system, security

00:31:42.090 --> 00:31:46.800
rules or updates, and
maybe package management.

00:31:46.800 --> 00:31:51.270
But for companies like Bitly,
for companies like Vimeo,

00:31:51.270 --> 00:31:56.430
where you have a strong
public-facing part

00:31:56.430 --> 00:31:58.410
of your business,
the public IP address

00:31:58.410 --> 00:32:00.850
becomes also very important.

00:32:00.850 --> 00:32:04.219
So I'm sure Kyle has
some thoughts on this.

00:32:04.219 --> 00:32:05.760
KYLE MOSCHETTO: So
Bitly's technology

00:32:05.760 --> 00:32:07.566
relies upon DNS,
which is arguably

00:32:07.566 --> 00:32:08.940
the most advanced
protocol that's

00:32:08.940 --> 00:32:11.080
ever been made on the internet.

00:32:11.080 --> 00:32:14.070
So we are basically held
hostage by the IP addresses

00:32:14.070 --> 00:32:16.560
that we've been
using since 2008.

00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:19.137
We have one Class C. We point
every customer domain-- we have

00:32:19.137 --> 00:32:21.720
thousands of customers, tens of
thousands of customer domains,

00:32:21.720 --> 00:32:23.830
all pointed to two IP addresses.

00:32:23.830 --> 00:32:28.620
So we are stuck in colo
providers and much more

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:30.295
antiquated systems
where they will still

00:32:30.295 --> 00:32:32.670
allow us to bring those IP
addresses within their system,

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:34.240
do our BGP
advertisements, et cetera.

00:32:34.240 --> 00:32:37.950
So we have been
leveraging and hoping

00:32:37.950 --> 00:32:39.660
that there is some
form of allowing

00:32:39.660 --> 00:32:41.970
us to bring our IP addresses
to a cloud provider soon.

00:32:41.970 --> 00:32:44.430
I'm going to just bump
over to Jeremy that.

00:32:44.430 --> 00:32:46.999
We really want that, and
that's a game changer for us

00:32:46.999 --> 00:32:48.540
because if we can
bring our IPs, then

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:50.820
we can actually do things
like go to the cloud other

00:32:50.820 --> 00:32:52.590
than just be our back end
services where we still

00:32:52.590 --> 00:32:54.390
have this long string
of network traffic

00:32:54.390 --> 00:32:55.170
that's going to go get data.

00:32:55.170 --> 00:32:56.910
We want it to go
right into the cloud.

00:32:56.910 --> 00:32:59.366
And we can't go to
10,000 customers

00:32:59.366 --> 00:33:01.740
and say, hey, pretty please
change those two IP addresses

00:33:01.740 --> 00:33:03.290
in your DNSA records.

00:33:03.290 --> 00:33:04.410
Come on over.

00:33:04.410 --> 00:33:05.490
That's a nightmare.

00:33:05.490 --> 00:33:07.420
Nobody wants to do that.

00:33:07.420 --> 00:33:10.080
And so being able to manage
that public space within a cloud

00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:12.430
provider is critical for us.

00:33:12.430 --> 00:33:14.100
And it's something
that we're hopefully

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:16.976
looking forward to soon.

00:33:16.976 --> 00:33:17.850
JEREMY JOHNSON: Yeah.

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:20.280
So he's ribbing me a little bit.

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:24.000
This is a feature that
we get asked for a lot.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:26.210
And it's something that
we've been working on.

00:33:26.210 --> 00:33:30.720
Obviously the infrastructure
at Google is very complex.

00:33:30.720 --> 00:33:36.730
And just being able to bring
in foreign IP addresses

00:33:36.730 --> 00:33:41.460
into our system and leverage
our connectivity to our peers

00:33:41.460 --> 00:33:47.500
is both incredibly compelling
and also incredibly difficult.

00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:51.590
So I would say, again,
watch this space.

00:33:51.590 --> 00:33:55.460
I think this is something
that we have been working on.

00:33:55.460 --> 00:34:00.220
I hear every day a new use
case for this functionality,

00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:03.350
so it's clear that there
needs to be an answer.

00:34:03.350 --> 00:34:06.390
In a lot of cases,
we've got we've

00:34:06.390 --> 00:34:12.400
got customers like Bitly who
have a single or a few very,

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:14.460
very well-known IP addresses.

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:18.489
And calling a maintenance
or a flag day and saying,

00:34:18.489 --> 00:34:20.340
hey, we're going to
change everything

00:34:20.340 --> 00:34:24.690
to a cloud IP address is
probably a nonstarter.

00:34:24.690 --> 00:34:30.010
We're also in a world where
we're multi-cloud now.

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:33.750
And I don't even know what that
means for maintaining autonomy

00:34:33.750 --> 00:34:35.973
over your own IP addresses,
but it's another thing

00:34:35.973 --> 00:34:37.139
that we need to think about.

00:34:39.719 --> 00:34:43.020
We've even had some
surprising use cases, I think,

00:34:43.020 --> 00:34:49.650
where traditional enterprises
might have supply chain or EDI

00:34:49.650 --> 00:34:52.860
connectivity to other
partners, in some cases

00:34:52.860 --> 00:34:56.370
thousands of other partners,
where those are, again,

00:34:56.370 --> 00:34:59.160
IP addresses,
public IP addresses

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:01.170
that are well-known
by their partners.

00:35:01.170 --> 00:35:03.660
And it's something
that they can't

00:35:03.660 --> 00:35:05.640
put their business
at risk on trying

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:07.150
to change those IP addresses.

00:35:07.150 --> 00:35:10.370
And I can relate one more story.

00:35:10.370 --> 00:35:13.500
At a company I used
to work for, we

00:35:13.500 --> 00:35:18.060
had some manufacturing
problems due to IP addresses.

00:35:18.060 --> 00:35:21.540
It turned out that there
were some certificates built

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:26.490
into the manufacturing process
that required a specific host

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:29.850
on a specific IP address
that was in a colo provider

00:35:29.850 --> 00:35:32.400
that we no longer had
a relationship with.

00:35:32.400 --> 00:35:37.470
So these are things that I
think we all probably have

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:39.060
an issue like this someplace.

00:35:39.060 --> 00:35:40.860
And if you look into
your infrastructure,

00:35:40.860 --> 00:35:43.530
there's probably someone
who knows about it,

00:35:43.530 --> 00:35:50.010
and through heroic intervention
has solved some problems.

00:35:50.010 --> 00:35:51.240
But these are things that--

00:35:51.240 --> 00:35:53.550
again, with all of this,
we need to automate.

00:35:53.550 --> 00:35:57.620
We need to build
networks based on intent,

00:35:57.620 --> 00:36:01.110
and we need to eliminate the
need for heroic intervention

00:36:01.110 --> 00:36:02.556
to keep our businesses running.

00:36:02.556 --> 00:36:03.930
OLIVIER VAUTRIN:
I wonder if it's

00:36:03.930 --> 00:36:07.402
useful-- something like this
is useful for Vimeo as well.

00:36:07.402 --> 00:36:09.360
PATRICK ANCILLOTTI: We
put everything on a CDN,

00:36:09.360 --> 00:36:13.450
so for the most part,
we're really lucky.

00:36:13.450 --> 00:36:15.834
Nobody has any need
to pin our stuff.

00:36:15.834 --> 00:36:18.250
So first time in my career
I've just been able to say, eh,

00:36:18.250 --> 00:36:20.020
CDN's problem.

00:36:20.020 --> 00:36:23.140
So while public addressing
is important for people

00:36:23.140 --> 00:36:26.920
to be able to get to us,
it's not a identity for us

00:36:26.920 --> 00:36:28.070
on the internet.

00:36:28.070 --> 00:36:31.854
We regularly spin up additional
CDNs and move stuff around.

00:36:31.854 --> 00:36:33.520
And so it gives us a
lot of flexibility.

00:36:33.520 --> 00:36:36.250
There are one or two
things on our network

00:36:36.250 --> 00:36:40.270
where we have sustained
TCP connections that

00:36:40.270 --> 00:36:44.770
require non-CDN paths,
but we handle those

00:36:44.770 --> 00:36:50.250
through other means
and they're not IP

00:36:50.250 --> 00:36:52.847
address identity-bound problems.

00:36:52.847 --> 00:36:54.680
KYLE MOSCHETTO: I'd add
one thing here, too.

00:36:54.680 --> 00:36:57.720
Does anyone in the crowd
have that IP lock problem?

00:36:57.720 --> 00:36:59.392
Is that a common thing?

00:36:59.392 --> 00:37:01.850
I'm asking generally because
we don't get exposure to a lot

00:37:01.850 --> 00:37:03.420
people that are like that.

00:37:03.420 --> 00:37:04.750
JEREMY JOHNSON: About 10 folks.

00:37:04.750 --> 00:37:05.625
KYLE MOSCHETTO: Yeah.

00:37:05.625 --> 00:37:06.330
Yeah.

00:37:06.330 --> 00:37:08.310
A good chunk, so
more than I expected.

00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:10.160
Makes me feel a little better.

00:37:10.160 --> 00:37:11.660
One thing I will
say about this is--

00:37:11.660 --> 00:37:12.810
and I'm going to bring this up.

00:37:12.810 --> 00:37:14.351
You may not like
that I'm doing this,

00:37:14.351 --> 00:37:16.712
but AWS announced last week
that they're doing BYOIP.

00:37:16.712 --> 00:37:18.420
And I freaked out
about that a little bit

00:37:18.420 --> 00:37:20.940
because it was like, oh my
god, that's the thing I want.

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:23.610
But there is a certain
advantage in GCP's environment

00:37:23.610 --> 00:37:26.220
to being globally
aware right away.

00:37:26.220 --> 00:37:28.500
The fact that if we're able
to bring an IP address in,

00:37:28.500 --> 00:37:30.125
or if we could move
to a Google IP now,

00:37:30.125 --> 00:37:33.190
we get global availability of
that IP address right away.

00:37:33.190 --> 00:37:36.780
It's out of the box and it
works with everything right now.

00:37:36.780 --> 00:37:38.130
AWS's does not.

00:37:38.130 --> 00:37:38.970
It's region locked.

00:37:38.970 --> 00:37:41.687
So it basically moves us
from one colo to another colo

00:37:41.687 --> 00:37:43.770
and doesn't really give
us much disaster recovery,

00:37:43.770 --> 00:37:45.690
doesn't give us much
hot hot capability

00:37:45.690 --> 00:37:47.010
to run multiple data
centers, or to run

00:37:47.010 --> 00:37:48.509
the same hot data
all over the world

00:37:48.509 --> 00:37:49.800
to lower worldwide latency.

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:51.690
So just some considerations
to think about

00:37:51.690 --> 00:37:54.360
if you are in that IP
lock scenario and you're

00:37:54.360 --> 00:37:57.020
looking for the little
things that you may not

00:37:57.020 --> 00:37:58.820
be thinking about
right now about what

00:37:58.820 --> 00:38:00.570
that means to move
that IP address around,

00:38:00.570 --> 00:38:01.890
because it's nuts.

00:38:04.870 --> 00:38:06.870
AUDIENCE: So is this a
road map item for Google?

00:38:06.870 --> 00:38:09.290
[INAUDIBLE]

00:38:09.290 --> 00:38:11.290
JEREMY JOHNSON: Is it a
roadmap item for Google?

00:38:11.290 --> 00:38:14.140
I'd say yes, it's a
roadmap item for Google.

00:38:14.140 --> 00:38:18.981
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:18.981 --> 00:38:20.730
KYLE MOSCHETTO: So the
question was, is it

00:38:20.730 --> 00:38:21.813
a roadmap item for Google?

00:38:21.813 --> 00:38:22.530
Jeremy into that.

00:38:22.530 --> 00:38:25.330
And then how subtle
was I trying to be?

00:38:25.330 --> 00:38:28.350
Like 50 on a scale
of 100 subtleness

00:38:28.350 --> 00:38:30.720
is what my goal was there.

00:38:30.720 --> 00:38:32.945
I'm just saying that we got
really far down the road

00:38:32.945 --> 00:38:35.070
in a lot of places and
realized at the last minute,

00:38:35.070 --> 00:38:36.986
oh, this really is just
a lateral move for us.

00:38:36.986 --> 00:38:39.480
It's not a feature add.

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:41.812
And feature add is what
moving the cloud;s all about.

00:38:41.812 --> 00:38:43.270
I can look at every
database I have

00:38:43.270 --> 00:38:44.910
and every big data
application I have

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:46.330
and say, gosh, when
I move to the cloud,

00:38:46.330 --> 00:38:47.919
things are going to
be so much better.

00:38:47.919 --> 00:38:49.710
And the same kind of
goes with IP addresses

00:38:49.710 --> 00:38:51.112
if you start looking around.

00:38:51.112 --> 00:38:53.070
But there's things to
just think about in that.

00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:56.090
So global is better.

00:38:56.090 --> 00:38:59.190
JEREMY JOHNSON:
Global is better.

00:38:59.190 --> 00:39:02.666
KYLE MOSCHETTO: If you've got
a question, go over to the mic.

00:39:02.666 --> 00:39:05.161
That's not the mic.

00:39:05.161 --> 00:39:12.606
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:12.606 --> 00:39:13.480
JEREMY JOHNSON: Yeah.

00:39:13.480 --> 00:39:16.680
So the question is multiple
public IP addresses

00:39:16.680 --> 00:39:18.650
per VM instance.

00:39:18.650 --> 00:39:20.670
That's also a roadmap item.

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:21.940
I'm working on it.

00:39:21.940 --> 00:39:23.490
So if you want to
find me afterwards,

00:39:23.490 --> 00:39:26.620
we can talk about it.

00:39:26.620 --> 00:39:28.240
So we're going to
run out of time,

00:39:28.240 --> 00:39:30.790
and I want to talk about IPv6.

00:39:30.790 --> 00:39:35.410
So before we completely
run out of time,

00:39:35.410 --> 00:39:39.010
everyone that is currently
running IPv6 on your network,

00:39:39.010 --> 00:39:40.791
raise your hand.

00:39:40.791 --> 00:39:41.290
OK.

00:39:41.290 --> 00:39:44.262
So 1/4 of the crowd.

00:39:44.262 --> 00:39:44.970
That's excellent.

00:39:44.970 --> 00:39:45.940
That's better than normal.

00:39:45.940 --> 00:39:47.440
It means we're
making some progress.

00:39:49.900 --> 00:39:52.910
Of the folks in the crowd,
how many plan to be running

00:39:52.910 --> 00:39:57.460
IPv6 on their network within the
next Year keep your hands up.

00:39:57.460 --> 00:39:58.050
Two years?

00:40:00.570 --> 00:40:02.401
Three years?

00:40:02.401 --> 00:40:02.900
OK.

00:40:02.900 --> 00:40:05.480
So that's still a little bleak.

00:40:05.480 --> 00:40:08.490
I'm hearing we need to, and--

00:40:08.490 --> 00:40:09.740
oh, when we need to.

00:40:09.740 --> 00:40:10.650
I see.

00:40:10.650 --> 00:40:11.420
Yeah.

00:40:11.420 --> 00:40:15.650
So it turns out this is a very,
very common problem with V6.

00:40:15.650 --> 00:40:17.875
And so I have two
comments to make about it.

00:40:17.875 --> 00:40:19.250
First of all, we
think that we've

00:40:19.250 --> 00:40:22.220
solved most of the
problem for folks

00:40:22.220 --> 00:40:25.310
who need public V6 with
our global load balancer

00:40:25.310 --> 00:40:26.600
support for V6.

00:40:26.600 --> 00:40:29.900
We do recognize that we need to
bring that to the network load

00:40:29.900 --> 00:40:31.790
balancer as well.

00:40:31.790 --> 00:40:35.390
But that would cover off
most of the public access

00:40:35.390 --> 00:40:38.420
to V6 addresses that we need.

00:40:38.420 --> 00:40:41.470
What I think is kind of
shocking and interesting--

00:40:41.470 --> 00:40:43.220
and it really relates
to this conversation

00:40:43.220 --> 00:40:45.890
that we've been having
about internal IP addresses

00:40:45.890 --> 00:40:47.750
or private IP
addresses-- is that there

00:40:47.750 --> 00:40:50.960
are a vast number of
enterprises who are just

00:40:50.960 --> 00:40:52.140
out of IP address space.

00:40:52.140 --> 00:40:55.290
They're out of RFC 1918 space.

00:40:55.290 --> 00:40:58.160
I see I hand, I see a
couple of hands, more hands.

00:40:58.160 --> 00:40:59.950
They're happening.

00:40:59.950 --> 00:41:03.260
And happens for
a lot of reasons.

00:41:03.260 --> 00:41:06.140
It happens because you
have a lot of growth.

00:41:06.140 --> 00:41:09.620
It happens because you allocated
IP addresses fairly liberally

00:41:09.620 --> 00:41:14.270
early on and now you're dealing
with a 10- or 20-year-old IP

00:41:14.270 --> 00:41:16.230
allocation policy.

00:41:16.230 --> 00:41:19.910
It also happens
through acquisitions.

00:41:19.910 --> 00:41:23.780
And so to solve this, we
get a lot of requests.

00:41:23.780 --> 00:41:27.050
We get requests to support
non- RFC 1918 space

00:41:27.050 --> 00:41:30.080
addresses in our cloud.

00:41:30.080 --> 00:41:32.600
That is also something
that we're working

00:41:32.600 --> 00:41:36.730
on that will be available.

00:41:36.730 --> 00:41:43.180
But the real answer to this is
a move to V6, and a move to V6

00:41:43.180 --> 00:41:44.840
is tough.

00:41:44.840 --> 00:41:47.930
And it requires
for an enterprise

00:41:47.930 --> 00:41:50.920
to really embrace this.

00:41:50.920 --> 00:41:54.020
I've got a couple of customers
that I talk to you frequently

00:41:54.020 --> 00:41:57.050
who are out of 1918 space.

00:41:57.050 --> 00:41:59.150
They would like to
move to V6, but they're

00:41:59.150 --> 00:42:02.930
one department in a group
of many, many departments.

00:42:02.930 --> 00:42:07.010
And the other teams that
they need to talk to

00:42:07.010 --> 00:42:10.070
would not be as
eager to move to V6,

00:42:10.070 --> 00:42:11.990
or they might not
be technology teams

00:42:11.990 --> 00:42:14.450
that can take that handoff.

00:42:14.450 --> 00:42:16.770
So we're hoping to
lead it in the cloud.

00:42:16.770 --> 00:42:19.850
And what we plan to
do is really focus

00:42:19.850 --> 00:42:23.420
on the enterprise use case of
the internal use cases for V6.

00:42:23.420 --> 00:42:26.630
And so things like VM-to-VM
communication, which you

00:42:26.630 --> 00:42:31.580
which you won't find on other
clouds, talking VM to VM.

00:42:31.580 --> 00:42:36.080
VM with internal load
balancers, and also

00:42:36.080 --> 00:42:39.920
the ability to talk back
to your premise over V6

00:42:39.920 --> 00:42:42.620
via dedicated interconnect,
partner interconnect,

00:42:42.620 --> 00:42:46.220
or over a VPN.

00:42:46.220 --> 00:42:48.590
So that's the stuff
that we're working on.

00:42:48.590 --> 00:42:50.278
I see a hand.

00:42:50.278 --> 00:43:00.595
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:43:00.595 --> 00:43:01.470
JEREMY JOHNSON: Yeah.

00:43:01.470 --> 00:43:03.210
So the question is--

00:43:03.210 --> 00:43:04.110
I love this question.

00:43:04.110 --> 00:43:05.730
You set me up perfectly.

00:43:05.730 --> 00:43:08.760
So the question
is, are we willing

00:43:08.760 --> 00:43:13.320
to consider a V6
gateway or a 6 to 4

00:43:13.320 --> 00:43:16.790
NAT that would enable
you to run V6 in cloud

00:43:16.790 --> 00:43:20.560
and not worry about what
other folks are running.

00:43:20.560 --> 00:43:22.480
And I think, from
my perspective,

00:43:22.480 --> 00:43:23.620
that's the right approach.

00:43:23.620 --> 00:43:26.160
I would rather take
an approach where--

00:43:26.160 --> 00:43:29.342
so [INAUDIBLE] --

00:43:29.342 --> 00:43:31.050
I want to call him
our CTO because that's

00:43:31.050 --> 00:43:32.091
what he feels like to us.

00:43:32.091 --> 00:43:34.590
But he's SVP of Infrastructure.

00:43:34.590 --> 00:43:37.620
And the way he talks about
this is the problem with V6

00:43:37.620 --> 00:43:42.680
is that it basically introduced
a new API without getting rid

00:43:42.680 --> 00:43:44.360
of the old API.

00:43:44.360 --> 00:43:46.090
And that's not how we do things.

00:43:46.090 --> 00:43:50.270
In every other in every
other part of development

00:43:50.270 --> 00:43:52.710
or operations management,
you don't just

00:43:52.710 --> 00:43:55.860
let two APIs hang
around at the same time.

00:43:55.860 --> 00:43:57.290
And so what I'd like to do--

00:43:57.290 --> 00:43:58.790
I don't know if
we'll be successful,

00:43:58.790 --> 00:44:01.560
but with customer support
we could do this--

00:44:01.560 --> 00:44:06.670
what I'd like to do is
to offer a V6 only option

00:44:06.670 --> 00:44:09.730
with these kinds of gateways
so that when you switch,

00:44:09.730 --> 00:44:12.080
you only have to deal with
one IP address family.

00:44:12.080 --> 00:44:14.260
You don't have to
worry about both.

00:44:14.260 --> 00:44:16.630
You don't have to worry
about securing V4 and V6.

00:44:16.630 --> 00:44:20.780
You don't have to worry about
these label abstractions, et

00:44:20.780 --> 00:44:23.110
cetera, with V4 and V6.

00:44:23.110 --> 00:44:25.930
And if we just
always do dual stack,

00:44:25.930 --> 00:44:29.810
you're always going
to have a V4 problem.

00:44:29.810 --> 00:44:32.910
And if you've already got a V4
problem, just by simply adding

00:44:32.910 --> 00:44:35.130
V6, I'm just adding to it.

00:44:35.130 --> 00:44:37.140
I'm not actually solving it.

00:44:37.140 --> 00:44:39.517
So I hope that
answers the question.

00:44:39.517 --> 00:44:41.225
AUDIENCE: No, I never
thought about that.

00:44:41.225 --> 00:44:45.121
But since cloud is
ephemeral, it's almost easier

00:44:45.121 --> 00:44:51.785
to just go out [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:51.785 --> 00:44:53.160
JEREMY JOHNSON:
So the comment is

00:44:53.160 --> 00:44:58.380
that due to the ephemeral nature
of most of these cloud assets,

00:44:58.380 --> 00:45:03.084
it might actually just make
more sense to fully cut over.

00:45:03.084 --> 00:45:06.290
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:06.290 --> 00:45:07.320
JEREMY JOHNSON: Yes.

00:45:07.320 --> 00:45:09.241
So there's a question
about extra bits

00:45:09.241 --> 00:45:10.740
and what you can
do with those bits.

00:45:10.740 --> 00:45:14.310
So interesting thing
about V6, and another area

00:45:14.310 --> 00:45:18.630
where IP addresses
weren't built for clouds.

00:45:18.630 --> 00:45:20.850
Most of you who
have been running V6

00:45:20.850 --> 00:45:23.670
are accustomed to using things
like stateless address auto

00:45:23.670 --> 00:45:25.740
configuration, or Slack.

00:45:25.740 --> 00:45:28.580
And that means that
you're burning a slash 64.

00:45:28.580 --> 00:45:31.800
You have 64 bits that are
basically reserved for the host

00:45:31.800 --> 00:45:35.170
to come up with its
unique identifier.

00:45:35.170 --> 00:45:36.630
This is completely unnecessary.

00:45:39.660 --> 00:45:41.760
It was actually a
transition mechanism

00:45:41.760 --> 00:45:45.000
because we have multiple APIs.

00:45:45.000 --> 00:45:47.070
In our cloud, we're
not going to do Slack.

00:45:47.070 --> 00:45:48.316
We're going to do DHCP.

00:45:48.316 --> 00:45:50.190
You're going to get IP
addresses in that way,

00:45:50.190 --> 00:45:52.200
or you're going to
statically assign them.

00:45:52.200 --> 00:45:55.440
And so the thinking
on this is, OK, you

00:45:55.440 --> 00:45:57.000
don't need 64 bits really.

00:45:57.000 --> 00:45:58.290
Come on.

00:45:58.290 --> 00:46:01.370
But we could still offer
you some bits to play with.

00:46:01.370 --> 00:46:05.720
And so what if we gave
you a slash 96 per host?

00:46:05.720 --> 00:46:07.230
And if you add a
slash 96 per host,

00:46:07.230 --> 00:46:09.720
you have 32 bits that
you can manipulate

00:46:09.720 --> 00:46:11.150
to your heart's desire.

00:46:11.150 --> 00:46:12.540
And that's 32 bits.

00:46:12.540 --> 00:46:16.140
That's the size of today's
internet on every host.

00:46:16.140 --> 00:46:20.100
So you could use those
bits completely on your own

00:46:20.100 --> 00:46:23.370
to encode whatever
process ID or service

00:46:23.370 --> 00:46:27.810
ID or anything like
that, and still

00:46:27.810 --> 00:46:32.710
preserve the very lowest order
bits for your host identifiers.

00:46:32.710 --> 00:46:35.670
So suddenly you get
the same benefit

00:46:35.670 --> 00:46:39.660
of this tuple of an IP
address and a tag all

00:46:39.660 --> 00:46:41.400
within an IP address.

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:46.140
And so this is an area that
I think this isn't probably

00:46:46.140 --> 00:46:49.200
something that the cloud
is going to solve for you.

00:46:49.200 --> 00:46:52.960
But it would be good, I think,
if we start thinking about--

00:46:52.960 --> 00:46:56.160
especially folks who are IPv6
usage practitioners-- thinking

00:46:56.160 --> 00:47:00.270
about V6 for cloud, not V6
for internet service providers

00:47:00.270 --> 00:47:04.760
or V6 for remote enterprises
or V6 for tunneling.

00:47:04.760 --> 00:47:08.240
OLIVIER VAUTRIN: Jeremy, so
perhaps we can ask quickly,

00:47:08.240 --> 00:47:11.549
how important is V6
4 for both of you?

00:47:11.549 --> 00:47:13.590
PATRICK ANCILLOTTI: I want
V4 and V6 at the edge.

00:47:13.590 --> 00:47:17.334
What I run internally is
not hugely important to me.

00:47:17.334 --> 00:47:18.750
KYLE MOSCHETTO:
Our infrastructure

00:47:18.750 --> 00:47:20.820
is not big enough where
V6 is a requirement.

00:47:20.820 --> 00:47:23.370
And we've yet to have
one customer come to us

00:47:23.370 --> 00:47:25.660
and demand Quad A records
or anything like that.

00:47:25.660 --> 00:47:28.900
So for us, we provide 301s.

00:47:28.900 --> 00:47:33.987
Not a ton of benefit
for IPv6 in that aspect.

00:47:33.987 --> 00:47:35.070
JEREMY JOHNSON: All right.

00:47:35.070 --> 00:47:37.320
Well, I want to first
of all thank you

00:47:37.320 --> 00:47:41.970
all for showing up at 9:00 AM on
the last day of the conference.

